<h1>Vocab 0026 — glTF Skinning Poses</h1>
<p>Terms from lesson 0027 (bind pose vs rest pose), session-9 issues #139/#142.</p>
<dl>
<dt>bind pose</dt>
<dd>The skeleton configuration a mesh was skinned (weight-painted) in — usually a
T- or A-pose. Skinned vertices are STORED in this pose; the skin's inverse bind
matrices map out of it.</dd>

<dt>rest pose</dt>
<dd>The skeleton's default stance from the joint nodes' own translation/rotation/
scale, composed down the hierarchy — what plays when no animation does. May
legitimately differ from the bind pose.</dd>

<dt>inverse bind matrix (IBM)</dt>
<dd>Per-joint matrix (<code>skins[N].inverseBindMatrices</code>) that transforms a
bind-space vertex into that joint's local space; the inverse of the joint's
global bind transform. The half of the skinning equation engines forget.</dd>

<dt>skinning equation</dt>
<dd><code>v' = &Sigma; w_j · G_j(pose) · IBM_j · v</code> — the weighted sum over a
vertex's influencing joints of "into joint space, back out in the current pose".</dd>

<dt>rest-relative delta</dt>
<dd>A bone matrix computed as <code>G(anim) · G(rest)&#8315;&sup1;</code>: correct only
when vertices are stored in rest space. Where the animation holds a joint near
rest, this delta is ~identity — the frozen-limb signature.</dd>

<dt>vertex re-bake</dt>
<dd>One-time load transform moving stored vertices from one reference pose to
another (here bind &rarr; rest: <code>restGlobal · IBM</code> per joint, weighted per
vertex), followed by re-uploading the GPU buffers.</dd>

<dt>CPU skinning</dt>
<dd>Evaluating the skinning equation on the CPU each frame into a separate
animated-vertex array (raylib's <code>UpdateModelAnimation</code>), as opposed to GPU
skinning where bone matrices go to the vertex shader.</dd>

<dt>shared rig / animation library</dt>
<dd>One skeleton + one clip set reused across many character files. Enables
implicit bone attachment: parts from different files posed with the same clip
move in agreement without any parenting code.</dd>

<dt>topological joint order</dt>
<dd>Parents listed before children in the joint array, so a single forward pass
can compose global transforms. Engines that assume it (raylib's
<code>BuildPoseFromParentJoints</code>) silently skip violating bones.</dd>

<dt>ground-truthing (asset debugging)</dt>
<dd>Verifying what an asset's raw DATA says (glTF is inspectable JSON) before
blaming any renderer — e.g. composing node transforms in python to learn the
rest pose has arms down.</dd>
</dl>
